.TH CHKBOUNCE 8 "2026\-10\-08" "chkbounce" "System Administration Utilities"
.
.SH NAME
chkbounce \- network packet reachability tester
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chkbounce
.B \-s
.RB [ \-4 " | " \-6 ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout= seconds ]
.
.br
.B chkbounce
.B \-c
.I server
.RB [ \-4 " | " \-6 ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-i [ types ]]
.RB [ \-t [ ports ]]
.RB [ \-u [ ports ]]
.RB [ \-r " | " \-b ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR count ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-\-shuffle [= seed ]]
.RB [ \-m
.IR key = value ]...
.RB [ \-\-timeout= seconds ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-csv= file ]
.RB [ \-\-json= file ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B chkbounce
determines which types of network traffic can traverse the path between two
hosts.
It operates in a client/server model: the server runs persistently on the
target machine, and clients connect to it over a TCP control channel to
negotiate and execute a sequence of probes.
For each probe the server opens a single socket immediately before the client
fires the packet, then reports the result back over the control channel.
.PP
Supported probe types are:
.TP
.B ICMP
Raw ICMP packets (ICMPv6 in IPv6 sessions) with a specified type number (0\(en255).
.TP
.B TCP
TCP connection attempts to specified port numbers.
.TP
.B UDP
UDP datagrams sent to specified port numbers.
.PP
Each session runs over IPv4 or IPv6, chosen by the address the client
connects to.
.PP
Both the client and the server need the
.B CAP_NET_RAW
capability for ICMP raw sockets, and
.B CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
to probe ports below 1024.
.B make install
grants both with
.BR setcap (8);
otherwise run as root.
.B chkbounce
must not be installed setuid root: it writes the files named by
.BR \-o ,
.B \-\-csv
and
.BR \-\-json .
.
.SH MODES
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-server
Run in server mode.
The server binds to the control port, then loops on
.BR accept (2),
handling one client session at a time.
It runs until interrupted with
.BR Ctrl\-C .
.TP
.BI \-c " server" "\fR,\fP \-\-client"
Run in client mode.
.I server
is the hostname or numeric IPv4 or IPv6 address of the machine running
.BR chkbounce\ \-s .
Hostnames are resolved via DNS before connecting.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-4 ", " \-\-ipv4
Client: resolve and connect over IPv4 only.
Server: accept IPv4 clients only.
.TP
.BR \-6 ", " \-\-ipv6
Client: resolve and connect over IPv6 only.
Server: accept IPv6 clients only.
By default the server accepts both families on one socket, and the client
uses the first address the resolver returns.
In IPv6 sessions,
.B \-i
type numbers are ICMPv6 types.
.TP
.BI \-p " port" "\fR,\fP \-\-port=" port
TCP port number used for the control channel on both sides.
Must match between client and server.
Default:
.BR 1234 .
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout= seconds
Per\-probe timeout in seconds.
The server waits up to this long for each probe packet to arrive before
reporting the probe as not received.
Default:
.BR 2 .
.TP
.RB [ \-i [ types ]] ", [" \-\-icmp [= types ]]
Enable ICMP probes.
.I types
is a range list (see
.BR "RANGE SYNTAX"
below) of ICMP type numbers in the range 0\(en255.
If
.I types
is omitted, all 256 types (0\(en255) are probed.
.TP
.RB [ \-t [ ports ]] ", [" \-\-tcp [= ports ]]
Enable TCP probes.
.I ports
is a range list of port numbers.
If
.I ports
is omitted, the default set is used:
.BR "22, 80, 443, 8080, 8443" .
.TP
.RB [ \-u [ ports ]] ", [" \-\-udp [= ports ]]
Enable UDP probes.
.I ports
is a range list of port numbers.
If
.I ports
is omitted, the default set is used:
.BR "53, 123, 161" .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-reverse
Reverse the probe direction: the server sends each probe to the client
and the client reports whether it arrived.
The server sends to the client address it sees on the control connection,
so reverse probes to a client behind NAT will generally not arrive.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-both
Run every probe in both directions.
Each probe is sent client\(->server and then immediately server\(->client,
and the report shows both results side by side, marking probes whose
results differ as
.BR ASYMMETRIC .
.TP
.BI \-n " count" "\fR,\fP \-\-count=" count
Sweep the whole probe list
.I count
times (1\(en127, default 1).
The report shows
.IR k / count
for each probe and direction.
A probe is marked
.B ASYMMETRIC
only if it arrived at least once in one direction and never in the other.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quote
Send ICMP error types (ICMPv4 3, 4, 5, 11, 12, 31, 40; ICMPv6 0\(en127)
as quoted errors.
The probe's receiver first sends a UDP primer datagram from a fresh port
to the error sender, creating state in stateful middleboxes; the error
sender then sends the error (code 0) quoting that primer, using the
primer's observed source when it arrived.
The receiver matches the error by type and quoted UDP source port, and
waits up to two timeouts.
Other ICMP types and TCP/UDP probes are unaffected.
.TP
.BR \-\-shuffle [= \fIseed\fP ]
Randomize the probe order in every round.
The seed is printed and recorded in CSV and JSON output; giving the same
.I seed
reproduces the order exactly.
Without a value a random seed is chosen.
.TP
.BI \-m " key" = value "\fR,\fP \-\-meta=" key = value
Record a tag with the results (repeatable, up to 32).
.I key
consists of letters, digits,
.BR _ ,
.B .
and
.BR \- .
.TP
.BI \-o " file" "\fR,\fP \-\-output=" file
Write the final report to
.I file
in addition to printing it to standard output.
The file is created if it does not exist and overwritten if it does.
Progress and connection messages are not written to the file.
.TP
.BI \-\-csv= file
Append results to
.I file
as CSV, one row per probe and direction, with the columns
.IR run_start ", " version ", " family ", " server_host ", " server_addr ", "
.IR client_addr ", " client_observed_addr ", " nat ", " timeout_sec ", "
.IR shuffle_seed ", " proto ", " number ", " quoted ", " attempt ", " order ", "
.IR direction ", " status ", " received ", " primed ", " probe_time ", " meta .
.I status
is
.BR received ,
.BR not_received ,
or
.B unavailable
(the probe could not be set up, e.g. the receiving port was in use).
A header row is written if the file is new or empty.
.TP
.BI \-\-json= file
Write the run as a single JSON document (metadata, per\-probe results for
each direction, and a summary) to
.IR file ,
overwriting it.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
If none of
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-t ,
or
.B \-u
is specified, the client probes all three protocols using their default lists
(equivalent to supplying
.B \-i \-t \-u
with no arguments).
.
.SH RANGE SYNTAX
Range lists are composed of comma\-separated values and inclusive
.IB start \- end
ranges.
No spaces are allowed within a range list.
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.TP
.B 80
The single port 80.
.TP
.B 22,80,443
Ports 22, 80, and 443.
.TP
.B 8080\-8090
Ports 8080 through 8090 inclusive.
.TP
.B 22,25\-30,80,443
Ports 22, 25, 26, 27, 28, 29, 30, 80, and 443.
.TP
.B 0,3,8\-11,255
ICMP types 0, 3, 8, 9, 10, 11, and 255.
.RE
.PP
.B Important:
When using short options
.RB ( \-t ,
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-i )
with an explicit range list, the list must immediately follow the flag with
.B no intervening space:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\-t80,443      # correct
\-\-tcp=80,443  # correct
\-t 80,443     # WRONG \(em treated as \-t (defaults) plus stray argument
.fi
.RE
.
.SH PROTOCOL
The client and server communicate over a framed binary protocol on the TCP
control channel.
Each message consists of a one\-byte type field, a four\-byte big\-endian
payload length, and the payload.
.PP
The session proceeds as follows:
.RS
.IP 1. 4
The client connects to the server's control port.
.IP 2.
The client sends a
.I negotiate
message listing every probe it intends to run.
.IP 3.
The server replies
.IR ready .
No probe sockets are opened at this stage.
.IP 4.
For each probe in order:
.RS
.IP a. 4
Client \(-> Server:
.I probe\-next
(protocol, port/type number).
.IP b.
Server opens the probe socket
.RI ( listen (2)
for TCP,
.IR bind (2)
for UDP, or reuses the per\-session raw socket for ICMP).
.IP c.
Server \(-> Client:
.IR probe\-go .
.IP d.
Client sends the probe packet.
.IP e.
Server waits up to
.B \-\-timeout
seconds.
If the probe arrives, it reports
.I received
immediately.
Otherwise it reports
.I not\-received
after the timeout.
Server \(-> Client:
.IR probe\-result .
.IP f.
For TCP and UDP the per\-probe socket is closed.
.RE
.IP
With
.B \-r
or
.BR \-b ,
a reverse probe instead proceeds as:
.RS
.IP a. 4
Client opens its own probe socket.
.IP b.
Client \(-> Server:
.I rprobe\-req
(protocol, port/type number).
.IP c.
Server sends the probe packet to the client.
.IP d.
Server \(-> Client:
.IR rprobe\-sent .
The client waits up to its own
.B \-\-timeout
for the probe and records the result itself.
.RE
.IP 5.
Client sends
.IR done .
Server closes the client connection and returns to
.BR accept (2).
.RE
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Start the server on the default control port
.nf
sudo chkbounce \-s
.fi
.SS Start the server on port 5000 with a 5\-second timeout
.nf
sudo chkbounce \-s \-p 5000 \-\-timeout=5
.fi
.SS Probe all three protocols using defaults
.nf
sudo chkbounce \-c server.example.com
.fi
.SS Probe ICMP only (all 256 types)
.nf
sudo chkbounce \-c 192.168.1.50 \-i
.fi
.SS Probe specific ICMP types
.nf
sudo chkbounce \-c 192.168.1.50 \-i0,3,8,11
.fi
.SS Probe TCP on a mix of individual ports and a range
.nf
sudo chkbounce \-c 192.168.1.50 \-t22,80,443,8080\-8090
.fi
.SS Probe UDP
.nf
sudo chkbounce \-c 192.168.1.50 \-u53,67\-69,123,161
.fi
.SS Combine protocols with a custom control port and timeout
.nf
sudo chkbounce \-c firewall.internal \-p 5000 \-\-timeout=3 \e
               \-i0,3,8 \-t22,80,443 \-u53,123
.fi
.SS Save the report to a file
.nf
sudo chkbounce \-c 192.168.1.50 \-t22,80,443 \-\-output=report.txt
.fi
.SS Combine all options including output file
.nf
sudo chkbounce \-c firewall.internal \-p 5000 \-\-timeout=3 \e
               \-i0,3,8 \-t22,80,443 \-u53,123 \-\-output=/tmp/fw\-report.txt
.fi
.SS Compare plain and quoted ICMP errors, five rounds each
.nf
sudo chkbounce \-c 192.168.1.50 \-b \-n5 \-i \-\-csv=errors.csv
sudo chkbounce \-c 192.168.1.50 \-b \-n5 \-i \-q \-\-csv=errors.csv
.fi
.SS Compare both directions of a path
.nf
sudo chkbounce \-c 192.168.1.50 \-b \-i \-t22,80,443
.fi
.SS Loopback test (client and server on the same machine)
.nf
# Terminal 1
sudo chkbounce \-s

# Terminal 2
sudo chkbounce \-c 127.0.0.1 \-t80,443 \-u53
.fi
.
.SH NOTES
.TP
.B One address family per session
Run the client twice with
.B \-4
and
.B \-6
to compare both families on a dual\-stack path.
.TP
.B Probe tagging
ICMP probes carry identifier 0xCB0C and sequence number
.RI ( attempt " << 9) | ((" type " & 0xff) << 1) | " direction ,
and UDP probes carry the same tag after the string
.BR chkbounce .
Receivers only count a probe whose tag matches.
This keeps kernel\-generated replies to earlier probes (for example an Echo
Reply sent back for an Echo Request probe) and late copies of earlier
attempts from being counted.
Client and server must be built from the same version.
.TP
.B One client at a time
The server is single\-threaded and handles one client session at a time.
A second client must wait for the current session to complete.
.TP
.B Privileged ports
Binding TCP or UDP ports below 1024 requires
.B CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
or root.
If a probe cannot be set up (the receiving port cannot be bound, a raw
socket is not permitted, or sending fails), it is reported as
.I unavailable
and not counted as received or lost.
.TP
.B NAT
When the client is behind NAT, the server sees the NAT gateway's address
rather than the client's private address.
The server uses the control\-channel source IP to filter ICMP and UDP probes;
a NAT mismatch can cause false
.I not received
results for those protocols.
TCP probes are unaffected because the NAT gateway rewrites the source address
consistently.
.TP
.B Version compatibility
Reverse probing (\fB\-r\fP, \fB\-b\fP) needs a server built with the same
feature; an older server ignores the request and the client will hang.
.TP
.B Host firewalls
A firewall on the server machine (e.g.\&
.BR iptables (8),
.BR nftables (8))
may drop probe packets before they reach the listening socket, producing
false
.I not received
results even when the network path itself is clear.
.
